WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.030
This technique is about creating high resolution
images from semantic maps.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:14.440
A semantic map is a colorful image where each
of the colors denote an object class, such

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:19.519
as pedestrians, cars, traffic signs and lights,
buildings, and so on.

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:24.380
Normally, we use light simulation programs
or rasterization to render such an image,

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:29.550
but AI researchers asked the question: why
do we even need a renderer if we can code

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:34.050
up a learning algorithm that synthesizes the
images by itself?

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.120
Whoa.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.760
This generative adversarial network takes
this input semantic map, and synthesizes a

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.900
high-resolution photorealistic image from
it.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.870
Previous techniques were mostly capable of
creating coarser, lower resolution images,

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:50.260
and also they were rarely photorealistic.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:57.190
And get this, this one produces 2k by 1k pixel
outputs, which is close to full HD in terms

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.530
of pixel count.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:03.640
If we wish to change something in a photorealistic
image, we'll likely need a graphic designer

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.210
and lots of expertise in photoshop and similar
tools.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:12.610
In the end, even simpler edits are very laborious
to make because the human eye is very difficult

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:13.610
to fool.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:18.280
An advantage of working with these semantic
maps is that they are super easy to edit without

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.530
any expertise.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:24.170
For instance, we can exert control on the
outputs by choosing from a number of different

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:26.850
possible options to fill the labels.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:32.000
These are often not just reskinned versions
of the same car or road but can represent

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.060
a vastly different solution, like changing
the material of the road from concrete to

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:38.060
dirt.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.700
Or, it is super easy to replace trees with
buildings, all we have to do is rename the

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.670
labels in the input image.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.130
These results are not restricted to outdoors
traffic images.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:51.800
Individual parts of human faces are also editable.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.190
For instance, adding a moustache has never
been easier.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:02:01.420
The results are compared to a previous technique
by the name pix2pix and against cascaded refinement

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:02.420
networks.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:07.190
You can see that the quality of the outputs
vastly outperforms both of them, and the images

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:09.580
are also of visibly higher resolution.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.060
It is quite interesting to say that these
are "previous work", because both of these

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:20.380
papers came out this year, for instance, our
episode on pix2pix came 9 months ago and it

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.610
has already been improved by a significant
margin.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.090
The joys of machine learning research.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:30.670
Part of the trick is that the semantic map
is not only used by itself, but a boundary

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:36.610
map is also created to encourage the algorithm
to create outputs with better segmentation.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:41.319
This boundary information turned out to be
just as useful as the labels themselves.

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:46.650
Another trick is to create multiple discriminator
networks and run them on a variety of coarse

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.300
to fine scale images.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:53.000
There is much, much more in the paper, make
sure to have a look for more details.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:57.860
Since it is difficult to mathematically evaluate
the quality of these images, a user study

00:02:57.860 --> 00:02:59.630
was carried out in the paper.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:04.989
In the end, if we take a practical mindset,
these tools are to be used by artists and

00:03:04.989 --> 00:03:09.950
it is reasonable to say that whichever one
is favored by humans should be accepted as

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:11.840
a superior method for now.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.319
This tool is going to be a complete powerhouse
for artists in the industry.

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:21.280
And by this, I mean right now because the
source code of this project is available to

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.730
everyone, free of charge.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:23.730
Yipee!

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:27.680
In the meantime, we have an opening at our
Institute at the Vienna University of Technology

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.740
for one PhD student and one PostDoc.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:35.490
The link is available in the video description,
read it carefully to make sure you qualify,

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:39.660
and if you apply through the e-mail address
of Professor Michael Wimmer, make sure to

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.790
mention Two Minute Papers in your message.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:47.481
This is an excellent opportunity to turn your
life around, live in an amazing city, learn

00:03:47.481 --> 00:03:50.739
a lot and write amazing papers.

00:03:50.739 --> 00:03:52.599
It doesn't get any better than that.

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:54.450
Deadline is end of January.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.880
Thanks for watching and for your generous
